17227268964,-0.3921041190624237,-0.2984235882759094,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.5386443734169006,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.007314567919820547,-4.60137271147687e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-7.152555099310121e-07,0.0,-1.6689286894688848e-06,-3.3378546504536644e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.010368647053837776,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-0.024522891268134117,-1.2159273865108844e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-2.002696055569686e-05,-3.6954811548639555e-06,-1.0227710008621216,-0.3030572533607483,-0.0001264730526600033,-4.768370445162873e-07,-1.6689286894688848e-06,-5.960462772236497e-07,-1.6689286894688848e-06,-0.00010144196130568162,-0.00020203932945150882,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-2.622600959512056e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.0002294515579706058,-0.0001245659514097497,-5.364403477869928e-06,0.0,0.0,-0.007809700444340706,-0.5019117593765259,-4.6491513785440475e-06,-0.00021991695393808186,-0.22663846611976624,-0.00013839241000823677,-5.054346183896996e-05,-0.024706894531846046,-5.400034933700226e-05,-0.000512111175339669,-3.576278118089249e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-9.595887240720913e-05,-0.005687009077519178,-9.536738616588991e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-3.814689989667386e-06,-3.814689989667386e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,279,19703,315,198,78045,89,61,18,284,220,17,488,220,17,72,59,60,1371,400,64,62,16,488,18668,62,16,4779,400,64,62,17,488,18668,62,17,4779,323,400,64,62,18,488,18668,62,18,2418,220,22603,400,64,62,16,264,62,17,264,62,18,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet the roots of\n\\[z^3 = 2 + 2i\\]be $a_1 + ib_1,$ $a_2 + ib_2,$ and $a_3 + ib_3.$  Compute $a_1 a_2 a_3.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ff005de370cff678c70e2b6ba7f332ae5f3edfb147f2e3fb77cf55d8c9c46af5"},"seconds":77.38616561889648,"seed":795622335514340989,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of finding the product of the real parts of the roots of the equation \\(z^3 = 2 + 2i\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Convert the given complex number to polar form**:\n   \\[\n   2 + 2i = 2\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Here, the magnitude \\(r\\) of \\(2 + 2i\\) is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   The argument \\(\\theta\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express the equation in polar form**:\n   \\[\n   z^3 = 2\\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the roots using De Moivre's Theorem**:\n   The general form of the \\(n\\)-th roots of a complex number \\(r(\\cos\\theta + i\\sin\\theta)\\) is:\n   \\[\n   z_k = \\sqrt[n]{r} \\left( \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta + 2k\\pi}{n}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta + 2k\\pi}{n}\\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n   For \\(n = 3\\), the roots are:\n   \\[\n   z_k = \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2k\\pi}{3}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2k\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right) \\quad \\text{for } k = 0, 1, 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the magnitude of the roots**:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[3]{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt[3]{2^{3/2}} = 2^{1/2} = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the arguments for each root**:\n   \\[\n   \\theta_k = \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2k\\pi}{3} \\quad \\text{for } k = 0, 1, 2\n   \\]\n   Thus, the arguments are:\n   \\[\n   \\theta_0 = \\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\quad \\theta_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\theta_2 = \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{4\\pi}{3} = \\frac{17\\pi}{12}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Write the roots in rectangular form**:\n   \\[\n   z_0 = \\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{12} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{12} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{4} + i\\sin\\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos\\frac{17\\pi}{12} + i\\sin\\frac{17\\pi}{12} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n7. **Identify the real parts of the roots**:\n   \\[\n   a_0 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\quad a_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\quad a_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{17\\pi}{12}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Compute the product of the real parts**:\n   \\[\n   a_0 a_1 a_2 = \\left( \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{12} \\right) \\left( \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\left( \\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{17\\pi}{12} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2\\sqrt{2} \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{12} \\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\cos\\frac{17\\pi}{12}\n   \\]\n   Using the product-to-sum identities and properties of roots of unity, the product of the real parts of the roots of \\(z^3 = 2 + 2i\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{12} \\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\cos\\frac{17\\pi}{12} = -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a_0 a_1 a_2 = 2\\sqrt{2} \\cdot -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} = -1\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
